Stream:

--> it was like a pipeline
through which our data passes through

--> when data passes throught the pipeline
there are various operations being happened like
sorting, filtering etc

--> useful when doing bulk processing

Collection ---> (step1) Create Stream ---> (step2) Intermediate
Operations ---> (step3)Terminal Operations 

at Step1:
streams are created from the data source 
like collection of array etc

at step2:
--Intermediate Operations like
filter(), sorted(), 
Transformation
  
from the step2 the output is also a Stream
these are 
lazy operations 
executed only when terminal operations are invoked


at step3:
--> Terminal Operations like
collect(),
reduce(),
count()etc... are used.

--these op triggers the processing of the stream
--and produce the output
--after this there are no other operations


For Ex:
when we 
have unsorted data
first it passes through step1 
collection is created 

now at step2 
the data will be sorted but still it was a stream

Java Example:
public class StreamSample{
  public static void main(String[] args){
    List<Integer> salaryList = new ArrayList<>();
    salaryList.add(20000);
    salaryList.add(30000);
    salaryList.add(50000);
    salaryList.add(25000);

    int count=0;
    for(int salary:salaryList){
      if(salary>=300)count++;
    }
  }
}

now using streams:
public class StreamSample{
  public static void main(String[] args){
    List<Integer> salaryList = new ArrayList<>();
    salaryList.add(20000);
    salaryList.add(30000);
    salaryList.add(50000);
    salaryList.add(25000);

    long output = salaryList.Stream().filter((Integer sal)-> 
    sal>=3000).count();
    System.out.println("Total Employees : "+output);
  }
}

Diff ways to create Stream:

From collection:
----->
salaryList.Stream();

From Array:
----->
Stream<Integer> streamFromIntegerArray = Arrays.stream(array);

From Static Method:
----->
Stream<Integer> streamFromStaticMethod = Stream.of(1000,2000,3000,4000);

From Stream Builder:
----->
Stream.Builder<Integer> streamBuilder = Stream.builder();
streamBuilder.add(1000).add(2000).add(3000);
Stream<Integer> streamFromStreamBuilder = StreamBuilder.build();


Different Intermediate Methods:
------

1.
filter<Predicate<T> predicate)
----->
Filters the element:
predicate is an interface
----->
Stream<Integer> salaryList = Stream.of(1000,2000,3000,4000);
long output = salaryList.Stream().filter((Integer sal)-> 
    sal>=3000).count();


Stream<String> nameStream = Stream.of("HELLO", "EVERYBODY", "HOW", "ARE", "YOU", "DOING"); 
Stream<String> filteredStream = nameStream.filter((String name) ->name.length() <= 3);
List<String> filteredNameList = filteredStream.collect (Collectors.toList());

2.
map(Function<T,R> mapper)
----->
Used to transform each element
----->
Stream<Integer> salaryList = Stream.of(1000,2000,3000,4000);
Stream<String> filteredNames = salaryList.map((Integer sal)->sal-1000);

Function is an interface

3.flatMap(Function<T,Stream<R>> mapper)
--->
used to iterate over each element of the complex
collection and helps to flatten it
--->


4.distinct()
---->
removes duplicates from the stream
---->
Stream<Integer> arrStr=Arrays.stream(arr).distinct();


5.sorted();


6.peek(Consumer<T> action);
--->
useful to see the intermediate result
which is getting processed


7.
limit(long maxSize)
---->
truncate the stream, to have no longer 
than given maxSize
---->

8.
skip(long n)
---->
skips the first n elements of the stream
---->

  
